window.docContents[63].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalE2E.HooksCommon/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalE2E___HooksCommon":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalE2E › HooksCommon.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the symbolic proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 246 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nDeclarations on this page (30)def heapBasedef heapSizedef HeapBlockFitsAtdef bumpAddr?def bumpThendef allocFaildef allocPanicHookdef allocReqdef allocHook0def reallocReqdef reallocHook0def allocZeroedHook0def deallocHookdef writeVecHeaderdef returnFreshVecU8def aesInputdef aesKeydef aesIvdef aesEncOutdef aesDecOutdef aesEncHook0def aesDecHook0def cloneSrcdef stringCloneHook0def formatInnerHook0def fmtWriteHook0def timeAddHookdef extraImagePanicNamesdef isImagePanicSymboldef withImagePanics\n\n\n\nCommon External-Call Hooks for libsignal-cliTrusted: the statement. External-call hooks for the Rust global allocator over [2^60, 2^61), AES-256-CBC (signal_crypto), String cloning and formatting, SystemTime addition, and image-local panic symbols. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.\n\n\n\nThe heap region and the lowest-fit bump\n\ndef heapBaseL34–37 ↗#Base of the heap region, 2^60: far above the CLI's image (at 0x400000), its mmap slots (from 0x6000_0000_0000) and its stack (top 0x7fff_ffff_0000) (DEV-M6, SignalSession/CliBinaryPq.lean).\n\ndef heapSizeL39–40 ↗#Size of the heap region: 2^60 bytes, so the region is [2^60, 2^61) (no wrap-around).\n\ndef HeapBlockFitsAtL42–46 ↗#Offset k fits a block of size bytes with alignment align in m: heapBase + k is align-aligned, the block lies inside the heap region, and every byte of it is unmapped.\n\ndef bumpAddr?L48–54 ↗#The lowest fit: the block at the smallest offset that fits (HeapBlockFitsAt), none if no offset does. It depends on m only through which bytes are mapped. (Classical: Unmapped quantifies over all addresses of the block, which Memory cannot enumerate cheaply.)\n\ndef bumpThenL56–63 ↗#Map a fresh block of size bytes (alignment align) at the lowest fit and continue with ok on the state with the block mapped read-write and the block's address; fail s if nothing fits.\n\nThe allocator\n\ndef allocFailL67–70 ↗#The failure of __rust_alloc / __rust_realloc / __rust_alloc_zeroed (their contracts' x0 = 0 branch): one allocFailedEvent, x0 = 0, return.\n\ndef allocPanicHookL72–75 ↗#A stub that panics after a failed allocation: appends allocFailedEvent, status Panicked, memory and registers unchanged.\n\ndef allocReqL77–78 ↗#The request of __rust_alloc(size = x0, align = x1) (and __rust_alloc_zeroed).\n\ndef allocHook0L80–83 ↗#__rust_alloc: the lowest-fit block of x0 bytes, alignment x1, its address in x0.\n\ndef reallocReqL85–86 ↗#The request of __rust_realloc(ptr = x0, old = x1, align = x2, new = x3).\n\ndef reallocHook0L88–94 ↗#__rust_realloc: a lowest-fit block of new bytes, the first min old new bytes of the old block copied in, its address in x0; the old block is leaked (stays mapped).\n\ndef allocZeroedHook0L96–101 ↗#__rust_alloc_zeroed: as allocHook0, the block filled with x0 zero bytes.\n\ndef deallocHookL103–111 ↗#__rust_dealloc: returns at once. Memory is never unmapped or reused.DEV-E6 (machine model): __rust_dealloc frees nothing. The real allocator may hand a freed block, with its old bytes, out again; here no block is reused within a call. A fresh block holds the bytes its addresses held before it was mapped (Memory.mapFresh). Why: a simpler allocator. Effect: w.l.o.g., argued: a reused block's old bytes are one more kind of residue, and outputs must not depend on uninitialised bytes. No theorem checks this (no CLI theorem varies the initial memory), so it rests on this argument alone.\n\nHooks returning a fresh Vec<u8>\n\ndef writeVecHeaderL115–117 ↗#Write the Vec<u8> header {cap, ptr, len} at v (vecHeader).\n\ndef returnFreshVecU8L119–128 ↗#A hook returning a fresh Vec<u8> holding bs in the sret x8: a lowest-fit block of bs.length bytes (alignment 1; for empty bs the block is empty and its address, non-null, is the dangling pointer of a capacity-0 Vec), bs copied in, the header {len, block, len}. If nothing fits: allocPanicHook (one allocFailedEvent, then a panic, as the contracts allow).\n\ndef aesInputL130–131 ↗#The AES call's input bytes (x0, length x1).\n\ndef aesKeyL133–134 ↗#The AES call's 32 key bytes (x2).\n\ndef aesIvL136–137 ↗#The AES call's 16 IV (initialization vector) bytes (x4).\n\ndef aesEncOutL139–142 ↗#The output of aes_256_cbc_encrypt on the call's arguments: the session model's aesCbcEncrypt.\n\ndef aesDecOutL144–147 ↗#The output of aes_256_cbc_decrypt on the call's arguments: the session model's aesCbcDecrypt.\n\ndef aesEncHook0L149–159 ↗#aes_256_cbc_encrypt(ptext = (x0, x1), key = (x2, x3), iv = (x4, x5)) with sret x8: if key.len != 32 (x3) or iv.len != 16 (x5), writes Err(BadKeyOrIv) (errTag at x8, zero allocations); else if 2 ^ 63 ≤ (x1.toNat / 16 + 1) * 16, panics (RawVec capacity overflow); else returns Ok(aesEncOut E s) in a fresh Vec<u8> at x8.\n\ndef aesDecHook0L161–194 ↗#aes_256_cbc_decrypt(ctext = (x0, x1), key = (x2, x3), iv = (x4, x5)) with sret x8:1. If ctext.is_empty() or ctext.len() % 16 != 0 (x1), returns Err(BadCiphertext) (errTag @0 and non-null &'static str placeholder 1 @8, zero allocations).2. If key.len != 32 (x3) or iv.len != 16 (x5), returns Err(BadKeyOrIv) (errTag @0 and niche 0 @8, zero allocations).3. If 2 ^ 63 ≤ ctext.len(), panics (RawVec capacity overflow).4. Otherwise allocates the output Vec<u8> before PKCS#7 unpadding: on OOM (bumpAddr? s.mem ctLen 1 = none), panics via allocPanicHook; on some pt, returns pt in a fresh Vec<u8> at x8; on none (invalid PKCS#7 padding), consumes the ctLen-byte heap block (matching alloc + no-op dealloc under DEV-E6) and returns Err(BadCiphertext).\n\ndef cloneSrcL196–199 ↗#The bytes of the String at x0 (its header's ptr @8 and len @16).\n\ndef stringCloneHook0L201–202 ↗#<String as Clone>::clone: the bytes of x0's String in a fresh Vec<u8> at the sret.\n\nHooks that allocate nothing\n\ndef formatInnerHook0L206–209 ↗#alloc::fmt::format::format_inner: an empty String (capacity 0, dangling pointer 1) at the sret x8.\n\ndef fmtWriteHook0L211–212 ↗#core::fmt::write: Ok (x0 = 0), the String unchanged.\n\ndef timeAddHookL214–229 ↗#<SystemTime as Add<Duration>>::add(self = (x0 = tv_sec, w1 = tv_nsec), dur = (x2 = secs, w3 = nanos)): adds secs (unsigned) to signed tv_sec and nanos to tv_nsec (u32), carrying 1 second when nsec ≥ 10^9; panics (Rust.panicked) if the resulting sec exceeds i64::MAX, and otherwise writes (sec, nsec) to (x0, x1) and returns to lr. Matches std's Timespec::checked_add_duration followed by expect, and agrees with SystemTime.checkedAddSecs when nanos = 0.\n\nPanic functions\n\ndef extraImagePanicNamesL233–236 ↗#Out-of-line panic stub <alloc::vec::Vec<_, _>>::remove::assert_failed not listed in Rust.isPanicSymbol.\n\ndef isImagePanicSymbolL238–240 ↗#Whether s is an engine or image-local Rust panic symbol.\n\ndef withImagePanicsL242–244 ↗#Extends hooks by mapping every symbol satisfying isImagePanicSymbol to Rust.panicked.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalE2E/HooksCommon.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalE2E.HooksCommon/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalE2E___HooksCommon"}});